  <article class="content-page section-space shell"><h1>A little care.<br>A better outing.</h1><p class="mt-6 text-xl">Start with the dog in front of you. Their comfort, condition and care plan come before the route.</p><div class="mt-8 notice"><strong>Sudden loss of movement needs urgent veterinary assessment.</strong><p>Seek care before measuring, fitting or choosing equipment.</p></div>
    <h2>Find the right support.</h2><p>A rear-support wheelchair depends on suitable front-leg strength, posture and skin health. Your veterinarian or rehabilitation professional can assess suitability and guide activity, fit checks and rest.</p>
    <h2>Make comfort a habit.</h2><p>Supervise every outing. Follow your care professional's guidance and the released product instructions. Check contact areas, fasteners and running gear before use, and stop if your dog shows distress or discomfort.</p>
    <h2 id="rover">A good ride starts before the first mile.</h2><p>Give your dog time to become comfortable with a carrier. Check that they can settle and change position. Plan for shade, water, ventilation and breaks, and check pavement and sand temperatures.</p><p>A running route, a shoreline and a ski trail each ask something different of the equipment. Use only the released configuration's stated surfaces and limits. Never leave a dog unattended in a carrier or use it as a vehicle restraint.</p>
    <h2>Choose the route together.</h2><p>A long-distance run for you does not have to be a long-distance run for your dog. Ask your care professional when riding is appropriate and how to balance activity with rest.</p>
    <h2>Keep repairs traceable.</h2><p>Use only the part revision, material and assembly instructions approved for a released configuration. Printable does not mean any material, printer setting or homemade substitute is suitable.</p>
